1
00:00:00,000 --> 00:00:02,400
I'm Yoko Hikasa. I'm Ayane Sakura.

2
00:00:02,400 --> 00:00:12,400
“Are you okay? Is your voice going to give out?” “No, I just overused my throat today.”

3
00:00:12,400 --> 00:00:22,400
“Wait, I brought something good for you.” “Really? Thank you! Perfect timing.”

4
00:00:22,400 --> 00:00:38,400
Hiyo keeps moving out of frame. This show is also broadcast with video on Super A&G+.

5
00:00:39,400 --> 00:00:51,400
“I have the old package.” “I bought it!” “No, you didn't!” This proves I'm the longtime fan.

6
00:00:51,400 --> 00:01:06,200
There's a new Mintia +VOiCE, Lemon Ginger, from Asahi. It's supervised by our old agency, I'm Enterprise.

7
00:01:06,200 --> 00:01:14,800
“This is so good.” “Right?” “Is it the same?” “They only changed the package.”

8
00:01:14,800 --> 00:01:26,660
I got the new package from my manager. “The ingredients are different.” “Saku-chan's has bellflower extract, vitamin C, and propolis.”

9
00:01:26,660 --> 00:01:38,900
Mine has throat-lozenge chips, bellflower extract, and vitamin C. The amounts are different, too.

10
00:01:38,900 --> 00:01:48,700
“Which tastes better?” “Let's compare!” I was shocked when I first got this—it was really delicious.

11
00:01:48,700 --> 00:01:59,700
“I'm Enterprise, you know!” “The colors are totally different.” “I finished my pack.”

12
00:01:59,700 --> 00:02:11,700
“Give Chanko one, too. He's still getting over a cold.” “Poor thing.”

13
00:02:11,700 --> 00:02:26,500
The old one is more ginger-forward. The new one has a stronger lemon taste.

14
00:02:26,500 --> 00:02:43,020
“I liked the old ginger flavor.” “The new one is sweeter.” “Bring the old one back!”

15
00:02:50,580 --> 00:03:08,540
The size is nice, too. It's about three regular Mintia tablets in one, and the flavor's really good.

16
00:03:08,540 --> 00:03:23,840
“Ginger's great.” “Everyone, try Mintia +VOiCE, supervised by I'm Enterprise!” “This is an ad!”

17
00:03:23,840 --> 00:03:41,720
“Why are we promoting it when nobody asked us to? We don't get anything from it.” “If we don't get paid, that's strange.”

18
00:03:41,880 --> 00:03:53,400
“Should we invoice them?” “No, we're doing this on our own.” “Is your throat better?” “It's starting to feel clear.”

19
00:03:53,400 --> 00:04:04,580
“Next week is Halloween. What are you supposed to do for Halloween?” “I just stay away from central Tokyo.”

20
00:04:04,580 --> 00:04:17,040
For a new radio show, the first year sets the tone for whether you join in on seasonal events. What should we do?

21
00:04:18,620 --> 00:04:30,340
“Let Saku decide.” “Me? I wouldn't do anything. I don't even think we need guests.”

22
00:04:30,700 --> 00:04:44,600
“Guests can come, but they can just stand there.” “I didn't say that! I'd rather hang out with Akari Kito privately.”

23
00:04:44,600 --> 00:04:57,280
Honestly, private conversations are never less fun than radio talk. “That's hilarious!”

24
00:04:57,280 --> 00:05:15,880
You can just talk with your close friends privately. “What about me?” “We're already teamed up on this show—we're in it together to the end.”

25
00:05:18,460 --> 00:05:34,940
I didn't used to do surprises or birthdays on my other show with Rina, but lately we've started dressing up for the cameras.

26
00:05:34,940 --> 00:05:49,100
“Then we don't need to do that here.” “I recently wore cat ears, too.” “If people want to see that, they can watch your other show.”

27
00:05:49,100 --> 00:06:10,680
“I don't want to see myself on camera.” “There are three hundred million people who do!” “Isn't that more than the population of Japan?”

28
00:06:10,680 --> 00:06:21,340
“Only about 120 million people want to see Hiyo in cat ears.” “That's everyone in Japan!”

29
00:06:21,340 --> 00:06:34,500
All right, we'll keep things normal—but Halloween food is allowed. “You just want to eat.” “I really do!”

30
00:06:34,500 --> 00:06:53,600
“Halloween falls on a Tuesday this year.” “So what?” “It's a broadcast day!” “That doesn't mean we have to do anything.”

31
00:06:53,600 --> 00:07:09,780
“But I want cake. Maybe purple sweet potato cake.” “We don't need to eat on the radio. You can't talk with your mouth full.”

32
00:07:09,780 --> 00:07:24,320
“Then we're not doing it?” “I'll accept food anytime.” “How about a high-schooler's recommended Halloween sweets?”

33
00:07:24,320 --> 00:07:47,880
Our high-school-girl writer once got teased for a mistaken tweet and felt awful. Producer Iguchi said a cutesy apology full of emojis would fix it.

34
00:07:47,880 --> 00:08:08,540
“Is ‘tehepero’ even trademarked?” “No.” “Can you still trademark it?” “People barely say it anymore.”

35
00:08:08,540 --> 00:08:25,760
“What do people say now when they mess up?” “Maybe just send an emoji sticking out its tongue.”

36
00:08:26,380 --> 00:08:39,140
“Is ‘tehepero’ just an old-fashioned way to say ‘Oops’?” “No, it's more like ‘Whoops, my bad!’”

37
00:08:39,140 --> 00:08:58,960
“What does the younger generation say now?” “Just use a tongue-out emoji.” “So emojis are still around?”

38
00:08:58,960 --> 00:09:18,980
“You use those on LINE, right?” “Young people don't use LINE much anymore. Instagram DMs are old news, too.”

39
00:09:19,720 --> 00:09:36,060
“What do they use now?” “Instagram?” “That's old, too.” “What's the latest?” “KakaoTalk.”

40
00:09:36,060 --> 00:09:55,760
“I heard people use KakaoTalk for affairs.” “You delete the whole app before you get caught?”

41
00:09:55,760 --> 00:10:14,960
“If someone has KakaoTalk, people assume they're cheating?” “Does everyone have it?” “It's useful in Korea.”

42
00:10:14,960 --> 00:10:34,320
“That's a convenient excuse.” “Does Chanko have it?” “He's the one here most likely to use it.”

43
00:10:34,500 --> 00:10:48,120
“Chanko, do you have a girlfriend? Someone you like?” “What was that pause?”

44
00:10:48,120 --> 00:11:06,500
“Does X have DMs?” “TikTok?” “TikTok has DMs?” “I think I heard people use TikTok.”

45
00:11:07,460 --> 00:11:23,380
“Everyone's on TikTok, then?” “Only the two writers here have it—Chanko and our high-school girl.”

46
00:11:23,700 --> 00:11:42,220
“Do you use it for work or just watch?” “I watch.” “Let's do some market research.”

47
00:12:05,140 --> 00:12:27,260
Shinobu still hasn't commented on episode two, but twice a week she tells me, “The views are going up!”

48
00:12:27,260 --> 00:12:42,280
“Is she a producer? She really cares about the numbers.” “Maybe she's connected to the show's publicity team.”

49
00:12:42,280 --> 00:13:03,780
She said the numbers weren't bad. Views usually drop after the first episode, but the second is doing pretty well.

50
00:13:03,780 --> 00:13:19,360
“I haven't even watched it yet.” “Where do you check the view count?” “Shinobu's keeping track, so everyone give it a boost!”

51
00:13:19,360 --> 00:13:32,980
“The second episode has about 100,000 views.” “That's great!” “The first had 230,000. How many subscribers?” “30,000.”

52
00:13:32,980 --> 00:13:49,740
“When does membership start?” “We applied, but we're waiting for approval.” “Hurry up, YouTube!”

53
00:13:49,740 --> 00:14:09,240
“The picture quality has really dropped.” “Don't zoom in!” “What was the first episode's quality like?”

54
00:14:10,240 --> 00:14:23,240
“It was still much better than A&G's awful video quality.” “A&G has video, too?” “Yes.” “Then we don't need it!”

55
00:14:23,240 --> 00:14:38,120
“Don't say that!” Maybe the video is for people watching live and commenting. “It's not about picture quality?”

56
00:14:38,120 --> 00:14:58,560
You can't zoom in or play it in the background on your phone, so you have to focus on that terrible picture.

57
00:14:58,560 --> 00:15:17,560
“Maybe that's fun in its own way.” “We'll get in trouble with Bunka Hōsō. I still haven't even gotten my building pass.”

58
00:15:17,560 --> 00:15:33,000
“Maybe they don't want you inside.” “But we can still call the picture quality awful, right?” “No, don't!”

59
00:15:33,000 --> 00:15:47,300
“Can we come to the studio three hours early?” “That's not something you ask on the air.” “All right, let's start.”

60
00:15:55,680 --> 00:16:12,600
This is “Hikasa and Sakura Won't Allow Digressions,” a talk show for two people who could chat for hours, but keep it to 30 minutes.

61
00:16:12,600 --> 00:16:27,720
Follow the show's official X account and use #Yodan. A surprising number of listeners knew Hiyo's “hororo” laugh.

62
00:16:27,720 --> 00:16:40,220
“Our longtime fans are really showing up.” “We're clinging to past glory—ghost hosts and ghost listeners.”

63
00:16:40,220 --> 00:16:55,520
“But we've grown up. We're on terrestrial radio at 1 a.m.” “Saku was 17 when we did Mojasaku.”

64
00:16:55,520 --> 00:17:15,520
“I was about 22 when I did DGS.” “Twenty-two!” “I'm not losing to my 22-year-old self.”

65
00:17:17,520 --> 00:17:39,220
“I could beat her in a fight.” “Maybe physically, but you've got more grounded now.”

66
00:17:39,220 --> 00:17:58,280
“Your radio skills might actually be worse than when you were 22.” “Don't touch the microphone. Yama-chan really will get mad.”

67
00:17:58,280 --> 00:18:23,580
“He'll perch on your shoulder!” “That's cute.” “Our director is a bird.”

68
00:18:42,380 --> 00:19:03,380
The new jingle has a soothing, healing vibe—like something playing at a massage studio or a sleep-aid YouTube channel.

69
00:19:03,380 --> 00:19:24,960
“We should loop it on YouTube.” “It feels spiritual.” “Maybe it could help people sleep.”

70
00:19:24,960 --> 00:19:42,000
“Yama-chan really likes the ‘hororo’ laugh.” “He made the jingle himself, and the last ‘hororo’ is his favorite.”

71
00:19:42,000 --> 00:20:02,660
“Let's read a letter.” A listener says they couldn't edit the Wikipedia photo of Ayane Sakura, even after trying.

72
00:20:02,660 --> 00:20:19,740
Wikipedia has protections against vandalism, so regular users couldn't change it. They promise to keep investigating.

73
00:20:19,740 --> 00:20:40,900
“Doesn't someone have stronger Wikipedia editing privileges?” “We talked about it before. Was that person called Satan?” “Don't turn them into a demon!”

74
00:20:40,900 --> 00:21:04,140
It seems you need an established account—registered for 120 days and with 500 edits—to change Saku's page.

75
00:21:04,140 --> 00:21:25,940
“Then edit Chanko's page 500 times.” “Mine probably isn't locked.” “Yours is definitely vandalized, too.”

76
00:21:25,940 --> 00:21:45,740
Anyone here with the right account? Someone even messaged my manager on Instagram about using a different photo.

77
00:21:45,740 --> 00:22:07,700
Apparently the subject or agency has to submit permission to change the image. “Why is Wikipedia so much trouble?”

78
00:22:07,700 --> 00:22:28,100
It's unofficial, yet it dominates search results and can look like an agency's official profile. Shows even quote it for introductions.

79
00:22:28,100 --> 00:22:50,340
“We love Wikipedia!” “Maybe if we say that enough, they'll help.” “I'll keep asking until the photo gets fixed.”

80
00:22:50,340 --> 00:23:24,480
“Use my official photo, everyone!” “Maybe in a year it'll be fixed.” “It's been wrong for five years already.”

81
00:23:24,480 --> 00:23:48,640
Next letter, from Crème Brûlée: “Hello, Hiyo and Saku.” “You abbreviated our names!”

82
00:23:48,640 --> 00:24:14,220
The listener tried to identify a restaurant from the second episode, using a photo of a painting and clues about Minato Ward.

83
00:24:14,220 --> 00:24:27,920
They think they found it in Minami-Aoyama. “That's terrifying. Please stop doing that!”

84
00:24:28,680 --> 00:24:46,720
“That's not it.” “This is another place.” “We'd have to ring the censor bell again if we named it.”

85
00:24:46,720 --> 00:25:12,700
“The place we meant isn't in Minami-Aoyama.” “Wait, have I been to this restaurant?” “Let me look it up.”

86
00:25:12,700 --> 00:25:30,300
“It's near the Aoyama-itchōme intersection.” “Aoyama Twin Towers?” “No, that's not it.”

87
00:25:30,300 --> 00:25:51,320
“The lighting is too bright for the place we meant.” “Crème Brûlée, that's not the one.” “I'm glad!”

88
00:25:51,320 --> 00:26:16,680
People really can identify a restaurant from one food photo—by the table pattern, the soy sauce bottle, or similar images online.

89
00:26:16,680 --> 00:26:35,820
“Google has reverse image search.” “You sound like someone who doesn't know how to use it.” “That's scary.”

90
00:26:35,820 --> 00:27:01,740
“Hiyo, be careful with your Instagram Stories. You post things like ‘Picnic at Inokashira Park.’” “I'm not that careless!”

91
00:27:18,780 --> 00:27:35,200
That's all for “Hikasa and Sakura Won't Allow Digressions.” Send postcards to Bunka Hōsō, 105-8002, or email the show.

92
00:27:35,200 --> 00:27:41,080
The email address is yodan@jokr.net. “Yodan” is spelled Y-O-D-A-N.

93
00:27:41,080 --> 00:27:59,440
The show airs Tuesdays at 1 a.m. on Bunka Hōsō, with a video archive Thursday at 9:30 p.m. and an audio archive on Onsen.

94
00:27:59,440 --> 00:28:15,180
“Hiyo, when will you get used to reading that?” “I've never seen you stumble during voice-over work.”

95
00:28:15,180 --> 00:28:28,500
There's also a YouTube channel, with video archives and after-talks for members. Please subscribe!

96
00:28:28,500 --> 00:28:46,580
“Are we wrapping up already?” “We ran out of time because we had too many digressions.” This was Yoko Hikasa and Ayane Sakura. Bye!

97
00:28:49,300 --> 00:29:10,880
“Hikasa and Sakura Won't Allow Digressions”—the YouTube after-talk. Today we tried sitting opposite each other.

98
00:29:10,880 --> 00:29:28,100
“It feels calmer this way.” “Calmer?” “Maybe I don't have to think about the camera as much.”

99
00:29:28,720 --> 00:29:46,720
When I notice the camera, I want to move around. I was moving more than ever today.

100
00:29:46,720 --> 00:30:06,600
“How does that look on the audio-only broadcast?” “The microphones are pretty good.” “Use the expensive voice-acting microphones!”

101
00:30:06,600 --> 00:30:21,440
“Those cost hundreds of thousands of yen each.” “Sell one on Mercari!” “Then buy another with the proceeds!”

102
00:30:21,440 --> 00:30:41,440
“Yama-chan's trilling again.” “He's cute.” “Anyway, Onsen has listener comments, right?”

103
00:30:41,440 --> 00:31:01,000
“Where do they show up?” “On the episode page, with the weekly update. The photo is different, too.”

104
00:31:01,000 --> 00:31:19,540
Last time, we forgot to write our comments while the show was fresh. Chanko asked us for them at the last minute.

105
00:31:19,540 --> 00:31:38,640
We couldn't remember what we'd talked about. Then Chanko stopped responding—he was doing a live broadcast.

106
00:31:38,640 --> 00:31:58,640
We both read his message right away and wrote comments on our phones. We're always glued to our phones.

107
00:31:58,640 --> 00:32:19,640
“We worked hard on those comments!” “Saku wrote a long one. I couldn't think of anything, so I wrote about Yama-chan's bird habits.”

108
00:32:19,640 --> 00:32:40,360
Chanko said our comments were too short, so we added three lines. Then he still didn't read them!

109
00:32:40,360 --> 00:33:02,640
We kept calling “Chanko!” in the group chat until the read count finally went from one to two.

110
00:33:02,640 --> 00:33:21,640
“Did you read it?” “I did!” “Will you praise us?” “Shut up, I was live!”

111
00:33:21,640 --> 00:33:40,240
“We're actors!” “Yoko and Sakura!” “How dare you say ‘shut up’ to us!”

112
00:33:40,240 --> 00:34:03,080
We kept vibrating Chanko's phone during his broadcast. He finally said it was noisy, then stopped replying.

113
00:34:03,080 --> 00:34:25,780
He turned off notifications and told us to wait. We said, “Don't mute us!” as if we were troublesome actresses.

114
00:34:25,780 --> 00:34:48,960
“We were live too—in the sense that we were living our lives!” “I've never heard that before.”

115
00:34:48,960 --> 00:35:09,840
“His presence disappeared.” “Let's keep bothering him.” “When should we read the next letter?”

116
00:35:09,840 --> 00:35:31,060
“Hiyo, you read it.” “Thank you, Tanaka.” “Why did you say our full names?”

117
00:35:31,060 --> 00:35:50,060
Tanaka is a third-year middle-school boy, writing in for the first time. Just using “Tanaka” as a radio name is impressive.

118
00:35:50,060 --> 00:36:09,380
Middle-schoolers usually want a cooler alter ego—something like “Sword Buster.” “Tanaka seems pretty mature.”

119
00:36:09,380 --> 00:36:31,260
He says he watched episode two in front of his parents, and our loud “sotto” jokes played through the speakers.

120
00:36:31,260 --> 00:36:53,720
“What should you do in that situation?” “You just went to your room and finished listening with headphones?”

121
00:36:53,720 --> 00:37:17,340
It's not an adults-only show. Think about it: the person saying “sotto” is the one whose parents might hear it!

122
00:37:17,340 --> 00:37:39,180
Tanaka has nothing to be ashamed of. He's the victim here; we're the ones responsible.

123
00:37:39,180 --> 00:38:01,760
“What do you do in that situation, Saku?” “Listen to the radio together?” “Other radio shows?”

124
00:38:01,760 --> 00:38:24,720
My mother, Shinobu, isn't comfortable with dirty jokes. We used to change the channel during even mild kissing scenes.

125
00:38:24,720 --> 00:38:43,500
But somehow we could listen to Anispa together. It was hosted by Masumi Asano and Takeshi Asaki, and had plenty of dirty jokes.

126
00:38:43,500 --> 00:39:03,940
My mother said Asano-san's jokes didn't sound dirty—they had a kind of clean feel. “What does that even mean?”

127
00:39:03,940 --> 00:39:21,120
“Healthy, maybe. Positive.” “Not that sticky, sleazy kind—more cheerful.”

128
00:39:21,120 --> 00:39:42,280
“And nobody gets hurt.” “Maybe some people could be hurt by our jokes.” “Only if they think we're talking about them.”

129
00:39:42,280 --> 00:40:06,080
“Everyone should be confident!” “No!” “Is this one pushing it?” “Today's a candidate for the edit.”

130
00:40:06,080 --> 00:40:15,140
That's the end of the after-talk. Please enjoy the main episode, too. This was Yoko Hikasa and Ayane Sakura. Bye!
